  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHigh clouds are expected to remain widespread across much of the \narea this afternoon. Cloud coverage expected to migrate east, \nleaving clear skies from west to east in their wake. The pressure \ngradient is 5.6 mb offshore from San Diego to Tonopah, NV and is \nexpected to trend further offshore into Thursday, before gradually\ntrending back onshore through the weekend. This will bring dry \nconditions and periods of weak to locally moderate Santa Ana winds\nThursday and Friday. Wind gusts look to peak in the mountains, \nfoothills, and passes at 35 to 45 mph, with gusts to 45 mph \nconfined to wind prone locations. In addition to winds, \ntemperatures will peak Thursday and Friday. High temperatures will\nbe 5 to 15 degrees above average, with the warmest conditions on \nFriday. West of the mountains, highs will be in the mid to upper \n70s to mid-80s. \n\nBy Saturday, most guidance has high temperatures cooler than Friday, \nbut still 5 to 12 degrees above average. More noticeable cooling is \nexpected early next week as a more persistent troughing pattern sets \nup along the Western United States. Guidance has trended later with \nour first precipitation chances. It now looks like precipitation is \npossible as early as Monday. ECMWF and GFS ensemble members continue \nto show mostly light rain for that time period. Snow levels look to \nstay above 8000 ft. \n\nA stronger trough that has the potential to dig south into Central \nor Southern California is expected Tuesday through potentially \nThursday next week. This system has the potential to bring more \nsubstantial precipitation, increased winds, and continued cooler \nconditions to Southern California. This system looks like it could\nproduce gusty winds from the coast to the deserts. NBM potential \nfor winds to exceed 35 mph is 50 to 65 percent along the coast and\n30 to 50 percent for the valleys. Mountains and deserts have the \npotential to see wind gusts exceeding 50 mph. This looks like a \ncold system, so snow levels have the potential to drop below 7000 \nft. There remains uncertainty in the details surrounding timing \nand precipitation amounts. NBM chances for 1 inch or more of \nrainfall Tuesday (Dec 1) through Thursday (Dec 3) are 75 to 85 \npercent for Orange County, the western Inland Empire, northern San\nDiego County and the San Bernardino mountains. Elsewhere in and \nwest of the mountains chances are 60 to 75 percent. The chance for\n2 inches or more of rainfall ranges from around 25 to 35 percent \nfor the coast and valleys, to 60 to 75 percent for the San \nBernardino County mountains. The chance for 3 inches or more of \nrainfall is around 25 to 35 percent for the San Bernardino County \nmountains.",
